  [1mB. Key-per-preference, no module[0m
  [38;5;153mlocalStorage.setItem("theme", "dark")[39m directly, read on load.
  - [3mBuys:[0m the least code by a wide margin — no parsing, no schema, no new file.
  - [3mCosts:[0m no version field, so a future shape change has to sniff individual keys; unnamespaced keys collide with
    anything else on the origin; defaults and validation get re-implemented at each call site.
  - [3mRules out:[0m clean migration. Once [38;5;153mtheme[39m exists bare in real browsers, moving to a namespaced blob means reading both
    formats for a while.

  [1mC. Inline the storage functions in [38;5;153mapp.js[0m
  Same blob as A, but no new file.
  - [3mBuys:[0m no new script tag.
  - [3mCosts:[0m mixes preferences with login handling in one 28-line file that has a single clear purpose today; every later
    preference grows the login file.
  - [3mRules out:[0m reusing the preference code from a second page later without extracting it first.

  [1mRecommendation: A.[0m The request is explicitly about persistence across sessions, which makes the migration story the
  part worth paying for now — [38;5;153mversion[39m and a namespaced key are the two things that are genuinely expensive to retrofit
  once real browsers hold data. The extra cost over B is roughly fifteen lines.

  Alongside that, one tooling question: this repo has [1mno[0m test runner, linter, or formatter, and [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no
  [38;5;153mscripts[39m field. Setting tooling up is cheapest before the code exists, and a storage layer with defaults, corrupt-value
  fallback, and a version field is exactly the kind of thing worth having tests for.

